  view show view1 V1 Coauthorship among Columbia’s 80 most prolific researchers, 2021–2024
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── answer ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
The force-directed diagram shows 208 coauthorship ties among Columbia’s 80 most prolific researchers in 2021–2024. The largest connected group contains 57      
researchers, a second contains 8, and 15 have no recorded tie to anyone else in the selected group. [G1, T6]                                                    

Jason Dennis Wright and Dawn L. Hershman had the strongest tie, with 85 shared works. Katherine M. Keyes had the most distinct collaborators within the group,  
with 17. [T4, T5]                                                                                                                                               

How to read the diagram: Larger circles represent more Columbia-affiliated works, thicker lines represent more shared works, and colors distinguish the         
connected groups. Gray circles are researchers with no ties within the selected group. Search for an author, hover for details, or click an author to highlight 
their collaborators. You can zoom and pan, adjust label density, or raise the minimum shared-work threshold to hide weaker ties without moving the nodes. [V1]  

Scope: Researchers were ranked by distinct works of any type on which they personally listed Columbia or an included affiliate. Ties count any works they       
coauthored in the Columbia-scoped OpenAlex snapshot, even if one of them did not list Columbia on that work. The snapshot is not a complete publication history.
[T1, T2]                                                                                                                                                        

Possible follow-ups: Which researchers connect otherwise separate parts of the largest group? How does the network change when only recurring collaborations are
shown?                                                                                                                                                          
